  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/... \nHeading into the weekend, the center of the anti-cyclone will shift \neastward towards the Four Corners region. As it does this, it will \nactually pull in some slightly drier air off the Rockies, helping to \nadd to what is already a relatively unfavorable environment for \nlower desert convection. It should not inhibit convection completely \nin the typically active spots, but coverage may be a bit more \nlimited. This \"drier' period should be short lived however as the \ncenter of the high will settle off to our north and east, inducing \nsoutheasterly flow once again and providing renewed moisture flux. \nRain chances for Sunday bump back up, and the lower elevations may \nget in on some rain well as PoPs increase to around 20-30%, some of \nthe better chances seen in a while. There still might be some \nrecovery time from the drier air needed, so it would not be \nsurprising that activity Sunday evening is not very robust for areas \nsuch as Maricopa and Pinal Counties, but it is a positive sign to \nsee an uptrend in rain potential nonetheless.\n\nFor next week, the forecast appears that it will not change much in \nterms of convective activity as ensembles have the high parked over \nthe Four Corners. Less subsidence aloft, and continued moisture flux \nbodes well for continued rainfall chances and that is reflected in \ncurrent PoPs sit between 20-40% basically from Monday through \nThursday. As typical with the monsoon, what days are active will \nhave heavy influence on what occurs the day after, so day-to-day \nforecast changes should be expected once we get into this more active \nperiod.\n\nTemperatures over the next seven days will be at their hottest on \nSaturday as current projections have widespread readings between 110-\n116 degrees for the lower deserts. Add the humidity to that and it \nwill not feel particularly great outside. Some more good news is \nthat as that temperatures will begin to \"cool\" as the high shifts \neast and weakens slightly, we may be able to finally escape extreme \nheat criteria by the start of next week, at least for now.",
